Answer from Github CoPilot : what is your take on mercurial

Short answer: Mercurial (hg) is a well-designed, capable distributed VCS with a cleaner mental model and nicer defaults than Git in many places. It’s less popular today, but still a solid choice for teams that value simplicity, consistent CLI/UX, and some built-in features Git only gained later or via extensions.

Why I say that — quick pros and cons

    Pros
        Simpler, more consistent command set and UX (fewer surprising defaults).
        Cleaner history/branch model (bookmarks/branches/heads are easier to reason about for many users).
        Good Windows support historically (fewer platform quirks than older Git on Windows).
        Extensible via extensions; useful built-ins like phases and a coherent approach to history evolution.
        Often easier to teach to newcomers because commands map clearly to common tasks.

    Cons
        Much smaller ecosystem and fewer hosting/integration choices than Git (GitHub, GitLab, CI/CD ecosystem, many tools assume Git).
        Less community momentum and fewer third‑party tools/plugins; hiring/knowledge pool is smaller.
        Some scaling/features (very large monorepos, certain advanced workflows) have received more investment in the Git world.
        Fewer modern hosting-first conveniences (pull request UX, code review integrations) historically tied to Git platforms.

When Mercurial makes sense

    You already have hg experience on the team and no pressing need to migrate.
    You prefer a simpler, more predictable command set and workflow.
    Your tooling and integrations are hg-friendly (internal tools, build systems).
    You need some of Mercurial’s built-in history-management features and prefer them to Git’s extension-based approach.

When to pick Git instead

    You rely on broad third‑party tooling, SaaS hosting, or an ecosystem that assumes Git.
    You need the largest pool of developers familiar with the VCS.
    You want features or performance optimizations that the Git ecosystem has driven (though Mercurial has competing solutions).

Practical advice

    For new projects, Git is the pragmatic default due to ecosystem and hiring. For established hg codebases, there’s often little practical reason to rush a migration unless tooling/hosting forces it.
    If you’re evaluating migration, consider cost/benefit: training, CI updates, hooks, large-file handling, and preserving history and metadata.

